TI  - Use of Registries and Prospective Cohorts to Improve Care
AU  - Pahys, J.M.
AU  - Marks, M.C.
AU  - Newton, P.O.
PY  - 2022
PD  - 
N2  - In an effort to combat the challenges of implementing and running randomized controlled trials for clinical research, the orthopedic community has championed the creation of large prospectively collected observational cohort studies to evaluate treatment and patient outcomes. These registries exist on a wide range of levels from single institutions, national databases, to multicenter international collaborations. The concept of large prospectively collected observational cohorts most notably began with the creation of the Swedish Hip and Knee Arthroplasty registries in the 1970s. Data from this cohort demonstrated the viability and usefulness of this model to improve patient care and served as a benchmark for the creation of a host of similar subspecialty registries within orthopedics. Today, registries and prospective cohorts not only provide information on patient-reported outcomes, but also serve to improve a surgeon’s individual performance through continuous feedback and the creation of best practice guidelines. Modern machine learning and artificial intelligence software are also being implemented to consolidate large volumes of raw data to identify patient risk factors and help guide treatment algorithms. This chapter will review the various subspecialty registries and prospective cohorts within orthopedic surgery today. © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2022.
