N2  - Infections of wounds have complicated the care of combat-related injuries throughout history.1,2 During the Vietnam War, there was a 3.69% early infection rate of 7106 upper extremity injuries and a 5.04% rate among 8838 lower extremity injuries, but this did not include the longitudinal care of the 68% of casualties that were evacuated out of Vietnam.3 A study of 84 open tibial shaft fractures evacuated from Vietnam to the United States revealed 1 of the 23 (4.3%) high-velocity wounds developed an infection, notably with Staphylococcus aureus, whereas 6 (9.8%) fractures associated with lower velocity injuries developed infections with Pseudomonas (3), S. aureus (2), and Aerobacter (1).4 Nine of 37 (24%) open fractures became infected in Operation Just Cause in Panama. 5. © 2011 Taylor & Francis Group, LLC.
