TI  - MILITARY SERVICE AS A RISK FACTOR FOR DEVELOPMENT OF AVASCULAR NECROSIS OF THE FEMORAL HEAD
AU  - Varzar, S.O.
AU  - Samarsky, I.M.
PY  - 2025
PD  - 
N2  - Introduction. Avascular necrosis (AVN) of the femoral head is a severe orthopedic condition that leads to ischemic destruction of bone tissue. Classical risk factors include long-term steroid therapy, alcohol abuse, systemic diseases, and trauma; however, in military personnel exposed to modern combat conditions, new determinants have been identified, such as prolonged marching overload, continuous use of body armor, trench-related hypothermia, and the consequences of COVID-19. These factors result in microtrauma, endothelial dysfunction, and hypoperfusion, thereby increasing the risk of necrosis even in young individuals without preexisting pathology. Objective. To determine the prevalence of avascular necrosis among Ukrainian military personnel, to evaluate clinical and instrumental approaches to early diagnosis and treatment, and to establish military service as an independent risk factor for the development of this pathology. Materials. The study was conducted in 2023-2025 in military medical facilities. A total of 336 men aged 20-50 years, with more than three months of combat zone service, were examined. Methods included clinical questionnaires, pain and functional assessment using VAS and Harris Hip Score, physical examination, radiography, MRI, and, in some cases, CT. In a subgroup, biomarkers of bone remodeling and microcirculatory dysfunction were determined. Statistical analysis was performed using x2test, Mann-Whitney U test, and logistic regression. Results. Signs of AVN were identified in 48 of 336 servicemen (14.3%). The mean age was 34.8 years, with unilateral lesions in 72.9% and bilateral involvement in 27.1%. According to ARCO staging, stage I was found in 16.7%, stage II in 37.5%, and stage III in 45.8%. All patients reported hip pain (mean VAS 6.5), while most had restricted motion and antalgic gait. Functional capacity decreased with advancing stage, with Harris Hip Score ranging from 78-86 in stage 1 to 56-66 in stage III. MRI detected early ischemic changes even in the absence of radiographic findings and demonstrated the typical "double line sign" in more than 90% of cases. Biomarkers revealed impaired bone remodeling: elevated D-dimer and CTX levels combined with reduced VEGF. The main risk factors were physical overload and body armor use (100%), hypothermia (87.5%), prior COVID-19 infection (33.3%), and steroid therapy (22.9%). Treatment was stage-dependent: conservative management and experimental MSC therapy in stage 1, tunneling in stage II, and total hip replacement in cases with collapse >2 mm or stage III. After six months, the best outcomes were recorded in the arthroplasty group, with a mean Harris Hip Score of 88.6 and return to service in 88.2% of cases. Conclusions. Military service under modern combat conditions is an independent multifactorial risk factor for the development of avascular necrosis of the femoral head in young men. The combination of marching overload, body armor use, hypothermia, and COVID-19 creates critical conditions for ischemic damage. Early diagnosis with MRI and biomarkers enables timely identification of patients suitable for joint-preserving treatment, while collapse >2 mm is best managed with arthroplasty. The implementation of screening algorithms in military medicine is essential for preserving the combat readiness of personnel. © 2025, Ukrainian Military Medical Academy. All rights reserved.
