AU  - Baumfeld, T.
AU  - Kuffner, M.
AU  - Baumfeld, D.
PY  - 2023
PD  - 
N2  - Stress fractures correspond to a bone lesion secondary to an imbalance in bone remodeling, which were mentioned in the literature by the German population during the Franco-Prussian war in 1855. Epidemiologically, this situation occurs mainly in athletes with ground-impact exercises, military personnel, obese people, and has a predilection for females. The case to be discussed represents an adult marathon runner without comorbidities, with a recurrence of stress fractures in the lower limb after the beginning of training for a street marathon. On physical examination, he presented with limited range of motion, pain, limping gait, and inability to maintain the training sequence for a marathon run. By means of the VAS (Visual Analog Scale) and AOFAS (American Orthopaedic Foot and Ankle Society) pain scores, it was possible to account for a score equivalent to 6 and 64 even though the conservative treatment was followed precisely. Conservative treatment was performed during the first two fractures with physical therapy, gait training, and rest until MRI-guided bone healing. However, after the third fracture located in the distal third of the fibula, osteosynthesis with a short intramedullary nail was chosen among several options, aiming at an early return to sports. After the surgical procedure the VAS and AOFAS score improved to 1 and 97. There are other ways to treat this type of fracture, although it is necessary to individualize each situation and evaluate the patient's functionality. The aim of this case report is to show the significant improvement in performance, the early return to sport and the absence of new fractures after surgical intervention for this recurrent stress fracture. © 2023 Elsevier GmbH
