TI  - A novel titania nanotube-based synergistic drug delivery system for combatting MRSA in orthopedic implant infection
AU  - Puli, S.
AU  - Sudhakar, S.
AU  - Selvamurugan, N.
AU  - Nallaiyan, R.
PY  - 2025
PD  - 
N2  - This research introduces a novel dual drug-eluting orthopedic implant to combat implant-associated infections (IAI) induced by Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA), an alarming problem in the orthopedic clinic. Titania nanotube arrays (TNTA) were fabricated by two-step electrochemical anodization. A dual drug-eluting TNTA system was developed, in which TNTA loaded with Daptomycin (D-TNTA) and coated with rifampicin encapsulated PLGA nanofibers (DPR-TNTA) to achieve synergistic antibacterial effects, aims to prevent MRSA infection during the first critical four weeks post-implantation. The antibacterial efficacy of the DPR-TNTA against MRSA was studied using in vitro experiments, including growth curve analysis, relative biofilm reduction, colony count analysis, and high-resolution scanning electron microscopy views of the biofilm architecture on the implant's surface. The surface characterization of TNTA, D-TNTA, and DPR-TNTA was analyzed using HRSEM/EDX, TEM, XRD, and ATR-FTIR. The MuseTM cell count analysis and MTT assay indicated that DPR-TNTA demonstrated robust cell growth with a higher portion of viable cells and an accelerated rate of cell proliferation without any cytotoxicity. The DPR-TNTA exhibited excellent wound healing activity, as studied by an in-vitro wound scratch assay utilizing the 3T3 fibroblast cell line. The hemolysis assay confirmed that the blood compatibility of test samples was safe for blood contact. A novel approach was performed to study the calcium deposits resulting from osteoblastic mineralization on DPR-TNTA using the Mouse mesenchymal stem cell line C3H10T1/2, which showed excellent calcium deposition. Our findings suggest that the DPR-TNTA system is a prospective implant material for mitigating MRSA infections and improving clinical outcomes in orthopedics. © 2025
