PD  - 
N2  - The emergence of new technologies in health and medical services is in increasing demand as they can combat illness and in various ways reshape the future of healthcare. The evolution of the medical device is greatly modified by the urging of patients, the options of doctors, and the realistic suitability of the design as forecasted by the engineer. One of the fast-growing fields is soft robotics technologies, which are being expanded to real-world situations. This presents engineers with countless advantages over traditional robotic technologies, eliminating the use of rigid parts. The most crucial application for soft robots is in the medical field, where they are used for various tasks like surgery, pain relief devices, artificial muscles, and so forth. The main objective of using soft robotics while designing micromachines for medical devices is the fabrication of robots using electronics and flexible bodies. But occasionally softness is limited to part of the machine. The properties associated with soft robots are deformability, leading to rapid restoration of their bodies. Exploration of the soft robotics approaches leads to the development of medical device design conducted towards promoting novel classes of devices, advances in wearable technologies, and patients’ specific approaches to medicines. There is great promise in the use of micromachines in medicine, but certain challenges are being faced in manufacturing micromachines using soft robotics. The application of micromachines in medicine has seen tremendous expansion, especially in developing new prosthetic implants, diagnostics, surgery, and therapeutics. Many soft robots could help medical implants such as pacemakers, real-time blood pressure sensors, augmentation mammoplasty, and drug delivery devices adapt to the body. The significance of soft robots is gaining importance in numerous applications in designing micromachines at a low cost. Future healthcare solutions that supply highly biocompatible, compliant, and customized medical devices to patients will undoubtedly benefit from the development of soft robotics in the micromachine industry. © 2025 by Apple Academic Press, Inc.
