TY  - JOUR
TI  - The need for porous bioceramics
AU  - Lukic, M.J.
AU  - Chen, S.
AU  - Li, B.
AU  - Xia, W.
PY  - 2026
PD  - 
N2  - Bioceramics become increasingly important with a constantly rising population and life expectancy but also due to pandemics and military conflicts, representing not only a medical issue but also an economic burden and a strategic means. Repeated medical treatments, such as revision surgeries due to implant material wearing, related secondary diseases and infections, and the patient recovery rate and postoperative duration, all affect the budgetary policy and interplay with a range of vital aspects of modern society. Depending on the intended application, bioceramic materials are supplied in different forms, such as powders, coatings, and dense or porous blocks. Following several generations of bioceramic materials developed over the last several decades, evolving from bioinert to bioactive grafts, contemporary trends shift toward bioactive bioceramics with multiple theranostic functionalities within a single implant material, bioceramic grafts that rather direct the physiological response than passively adapt to it, which is altogether a challenging task that requires a multidisciplinary approach at the interface of materials science, chemistry, physics, engineering, biology, and medicine. Bioceramic materials can be subjected to diverse structural and chemical optimization strategies to enhance their biocompatibility, bioactivity, osteoinductivity, osteoconductivity, and osteogenic and vascularization potential, which is at the forefront for targeting specific needs in orthopedics and dentistry. Advancements in nanotechnology, additive manufacturing, the emergence of artificial intelligence (AI), and the understanding of underpinning cellular mechanisms and gene regulation have greatly improved the field of implant materials for bone and dental tissue restoration and regeneration, toward personalized implant design and optimization. However, the discrepancy between clinical needs and the availability of adequate bioceramic materials does not seem to narrow so fast. The analysis of the publication outputs from the SCOPUS database from 2010 to 2025 shows a high number of publications focusing on “porous bioceramics,” suggesting a continuously rising interest in the field. We witness the strong research focus on novel approaches toward standardized and optimized solutions, for example, designed hierarchical porosity, protein functionalization of bioceramic surfaces, mineralization of (nonnecessarily bioceramic) scaffolds, precise surface chemistry adjustments, implementation of organic-inorganic hybrid materials, etc. Still, the dynamics of their translation into newly appearing products are hardly foreseeable, but they are certainly expected to greatly improve the field. Moreover, the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in optimizing bioceramic constructs is yet to shine its full potential, for example, in designing stimuli-responsive formulations with appropriate porosity by adjusting the chemical composition and printing strategies, which may revolutionize the current landscape of bone and dental regenerative medicine. © 2026 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.
