N2  - Medical workers from the pediatric orthopaedics department usually are not considered as frontline staff, but they still have a high risk of infection, especially when they have to work as backup staff in fever clinics or the emergency department during a pandemic. In all, 13 medical workers from the pediatric orthopaedics department were confirmed to have COVID-19 during the pandemic (Table IV). However, none of them had been treating pediatric orthopaedic patients when the infection was confirmed. The same perioperative strategies were adopted for adult patients with COVID-1919. After adoption of these strategies, no medical worker had a confirmed infection, which is a testament to the efficacy of our strategies. More attention should be paid to the nosocomial infection rate among medical workers since fatigue and insufficient PPE are the predominant factors that contributed to COVID-19 infection in orthopaedic surgeons in Wuhan20. These experiences from Wuhan might prove valuable for pediatric orthopaedic colleagues around the world in the ongoing war against COVID-19. © 2020 BY THE JOURNAL OF BONE AND JOINT SURGERY, INCORPORATED
