TI  - What place for maggot therapy in the treatment of diabetic foot wounds?
AU  - Schlienger, J.-L.
PY  - 2021
PD  - 
N2  - The beneficial effects of maggot-infested wounds were noted by military surgeons for centuries. After the first World War, William Bear, an orthopedic surgeon at Johns Hopkins Hospital, was the first to apply maggots systematically to nonhealing wounds. The benefits of maggot therapy were discovered around the world particularly for feet diabetic ulcers treatment. The renaissance in maggot therapy is due in large part to recent technological advancements: simplified application of this perishable medical device, simplified application, modern dressing materials minimizing the risk of escaping maggots. The efficacy and safety of maggot therapy for diabetic foot ulcer is now demonstrated. Today it is accepted that medicinal maggots are an efficacious alternative therapy for the debridement and the care of diabetic ulcers. © 2020 Elsevier Masson SAS
