TY  - JOUR
TI  - What? No Lab? Preparing the Patient for Operation
AU  - Contini, S.
PY  - 2016
PD  - 
N2  - In resource-limited settings, health facilities and peripheral hospitals have usually minimal or absent laboratory support. In more remote areas, piped water and power supply are not available constantly and spaces for labs are limited. Moreover, the few basic laboratory tests available are sometimes unreliable. Local physicians often perceive these tests as unhelpful, such that they remain underutilized and undervalued. Therefore, evaluation of the patients for surgery and treatment planning must be basically founded on good history taking, watchful clinical examination, and sound experience, keeping in mind that these patients are significantly different from those we may be used to treat with in western countries, being affected by the consequences of war or natural disaster, malnutrition, poor quality water, infectious diseases, dehydration, etc. Only rarely surgery will not be performed for anesthetic reasons. If surgery is deemed unavoidable, delay is only beneficial if the patient’s condition can be improved without waiting for more elaborate tests. Accordingly, laboratory investigations in austere environment should only be used if their results will influence the therapeutic plan. Hemoglobin, serum glucose, and blood group are valuable in most patients before surgery. Electrolytes (Na, K) and liver or kidney tests are not mandatory. ECG or chest radiology is not routinely performed unless there is a specific indication. Blood should be transfused only for lifesaving indications. A lesson from austere environment is that patients can survive at very low hemoglobin levels, provided they are well oxygenated and filled. The provision of blood is often problematic and unreliable in austere settings, with a substantial risk of disease transmission. Wounds cultures are not recommended being challenging and often unreliable. In high-risk patients, thromboprophylaxis with low weight heparin is probably justified on a selected base. In conclusion, orthopedic trauma care can be reasonably managed in austere conditions, in spite of the scarcity of sometimes complex laboratory tests, meeting satisfactorily the clinical needs and achieving an acceptable outcome. The majority of lab investigations do not add too much to the surgical plan and to the quality of surgical care in such environment. © Springer International Publishing Switzerland 2016.
