TI  - History of reconstructive operations
AU  - Gohritz, A.
AU  - Langer, M.
PY  - 2021
PD  - 
N2  - The review of the historical development of functional reconstructive surgery using tendon and nerve transfers in the period between the end of the nineteenth and the beginning of the twentieth centuries provides a fascinating insight into the beginnings of orthopaedic and surgical specialties and the pioneering achievements of our predecessors. They founded many of the principles of these operative procedures that are still valid today. The technical development was significantly influenced by social and societal changes and contemporary historical events. The decisive factor was often the tireless commitment of individuals who thought ahead of their time and whom their contemporaries often met with scepticism or resistance. The historical analysis of the beginnings of motor and nerve replacement surgery shows us the ingenuity, endurance and accuracy of our predecessors, who can serve as inspiration and role models in our daily work and in looking into the future of functional reconstructive surgery. © Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2021. All rights reserved.
