TI  - The windblown hand and its surgical management
AU  - Neser, C.
AU  - Graewe, F.R.
AU  - Carter, S.L.
PY  - 2016
PD  - 
N2  - Purpose: The windblown hand is rare congenital abnormality of the hand, which was first described in 1897 by Emile Boix. It is characterised by thumb flexion and adduction contracture, a narrowed first web space, ulnar deviation and flexion of the digits. The purpose of the study is to report on the surgical management of the windblown hand and to propose a surgical decision making algorithm. Method: The authors performed a retrospective chart review of all patients with windblown hand treated at Red Cross War Memorial Children’s Hospital between June 2004 and April 2012. We describe the surgical decision making algorithm and report on the management and outcomes of 11 windblown hands in six patients. Results: In our series, 5 males and 1 female had bilateral hand deformities. 11 hands in the 6 patients underwent surgical correction. The median age of surgery to the first hand was 23 months (range 14-29 months). The contralateral hand was operated on at a median age of 37 months (range 22-91 months). Postoperative outcomes were good or excellent in 9 of the 11 operated hands. Minor complications occurred in 2 patients and there were no major complications. Conclusion: The surgical management of the windblown hand is simplified by using a surgical decision making algorithm based on the observed anatomical abnormalities and results in good functional and cosmetic outcomes in the windblown hand. © 2016 Taylor & Francis.
