TI  - In-Vitro and In-Vivo Models for the Study of Prosthetic Joint Infections
AU  - Mannering, N.
AU  - Narulla, R.
AU  - Lenane, B.
PY  - 2021
PD  - 
N2  - In-vitro and in-vivo models for the study of prosthetic joint infection (PJI) have developed significantly over the past few decades. Due to the projected rise in primary and revision arthroplasties around the world, reliable and relevant in-vitro and in-vivo models will be necessary to understand and combat the rising burden of PJI disease. The first half of this chapter will review in-vitro studies on the pathogenesis of common PJI bacteria, including adherence mechanisms and biofilm, then address well-recognized antibiotic treatment options. The second half will focus on types of in-vivo studies, including animal models, prosthetic designs, and antibiotic regimes, to replicate the environment surrounding PJI in human total knee arthroplasty. It will also be shown that, despite their limitations, preclinical trials are necessary to advance our understanding in a safe and ethical way. Both in-vitro and in-vivo models will need to take advantage of new technologies as they develop, with the aim of answering the difficult questions of why PJI occurs and how it can be managed. This will allow preclinical models to become increasingly more relevant and applicable to this complex disease as it is experienced by orthopaedic surgeons. © ISAKOS 2022.
