TI  - Custom External Coaptation as a Pain Management Tool: Veterinary Orthotics and Prosthetics
AU  - Kaufmann, M.W.
AU  - Mich, P.M.
PY  - 2013
PD  - 
N2  - Modern human orthotics and prosthetics (H-OP) has been advanced by experiences gained from treating catastrophic wartime injury, sports injury, complications of diabetes, and congenital limb deformities. The techniques and materials used in H-OP are now being utilized in veterinary medicine. Surgical management of many orthopedic conditions in veterinary species remains the standard of care and the preferred therapeutic choice. Custom external coaptation can be used to provide security before and after the surgical repair and help prevent wounds or surgical failures caused by splinting, wet bandages, splint material fatigue/breakdown, and lack of patient tolerance of a support splint. Patient evaluation for a veterinary-OP (V-OP) device must be thorough and incorporate at least five separate examinations in order to fully define the presenting deficit, characterize biomechanical implications, identify comorbidities and potential complicators, and diagnose all primary and secondary pain generators. © 2014 by John Wiley & Sons, Inc.
