TI  - External fixation about the knee in high-energy war injuries
AU  - Vining, N.C.
AU  - Pasque, C.B.
PY  - 2010
PD  - 
N2  - External fixation for the treatment of musculoskeletal injuries in the current military conflicts has emerged as a vital part of the treatment of high-energy war injuries, including those involving the knee. Spanning fixators are a simple, safe, and rapid way to obtain stability and alignment about the knee during the transport and evacuation phases of the care of wounded military personnel. This provides a much needed damage control technique for the overall resuscitation and comfort of the injured military personnel as well helping to stabilize the bone and soft tissue injuries for future definitive fixation and reconstructive procedures. In addition, external fixation about the knee has become a valuable tool as the definitive treatment for these high-energy injuries about the knee for the local population where limited resources are available for more definitive procedures. This article discusses important external fixation treatment principles that can be applied to civilian practices for the temporary and sometimes definitive care of fractures about the knee. Copyright © 2010 by Lippincott Williams & Wilkins.
