TI  - Burns and Extremity Injury: A Difficult Combination
AU  - Hayda, R.
PY  - 2014
PD  - 
N2  - On the front line, casualties with burns requiring care may be seen among warfighters as well as the local civilian population. Burns associated with fracture or other extremity injury can be very challenging to the orthopedic surgeon who gets limited exposure if any to burn patients during their training and practice. However, in the deployed setting or in humanitarian missions, the orthopedic surgeon may be required to treat these patients. Analysis of conflicts in the past century has shown that burn victims form about 5 % of war casualties on the modern battlefield. Similarly, burn injury is common in the developing world and conflict areas where cooking and heating needs are met with open flame devices exposing family members especially children to the potential of burns. For these reasons the orthopedic surgeon on the front line must be familiar in burn treatment principles. © Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2014.
