TI  - Management of lower limbs amputees in Orthopaedic Centre of Kalembe-lembe in Kinshasa: A retrospective study over 6 years
AU  - Muzembo Ndundu, J.
AU  - Motumbe Likita, B.
PY  - 2012
PD  - 
N2  - Objective: To report the frequency of cases of lower limbs amputation in the years 2004 through 2009 in Democratic Republic of the Congo. To investigate the causes of injuries that justify amputation and analyse the care by physiotherapy and rehabilitation. Patients and method: This retrospective study identified over six years, 1442 cases of lower limbs amputations, performed at the Orthopaedic Centre of Kalembe-lembe. Of these, 110 cases were selected to be followed in rehabilitation. An analysis of different aetiologies causing amputations was performed on this sample of 110 cases. Finally, the various means of rehabilitation of these patients were observed and criticised. Results: In six years, a large annual increase in the number of lower limbs amputation was found. Whereas 176 patients were operated on in 2004, this number has almost doubled in six years, rising to 317 cases, an 80 % increase. Moreover, from the analysis of 110 cases of amputations, we found that 90 patients were male and 20 female. Also, 71 patients were military and 39 civilians. The majority of patients were aged 20 to 50 years. The most common cause of the lower limbs amputations is the accidents of war, followed by accidents of the public highway. It appears in this study that men seem more at risk of amputation than women. Conclusion: The steady increase in lower limbs amputations in these 6 years is one cause of disability among middle-aged patients in Kinshasa. This is particularly due to the war experienced by the country. Rehabilitation is an essential mean to limit the functional impact of amputations, to increase autonomy of amputees and facilitate their social reintegration. © 2012 Published by Elsevier Masson SAS.
