TI  - Relationship of osteoprotegerin and receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B ligand with postmenopausal osteoporosis syndrome types in traditional Chinese medicine
AU  - Huang, H.-X.
AU  - Wan, L.
AU  - Deng, W.M.
AU  - Liu, C.J.
PY  - 2007
PD  - 
N2  - Aim: To investigate the variable inherent relationships of osteoprotegerin and receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B ligand in the different syndrome types of postmenopausal osteoporosis patients, and provide theoretical and experimental evidence for the differentiation of symptoms and signs and classification of syndrome of postmenopausal osteoporosis. Methods: 108 inpatients and outpatients with postmenopausal osteoporosis treated in Orthopedics and Trauma Hospital, Guangzhou University of Traditional Chinese Medicine, and the General Hospital of Guangzhou Military Area Command of Chinese PLA were selected from September 2005 to September 2006. According to the principles of the differentiation of syndromes in traditional Chinese medicine, they were divided into four syndrome types, namely deficiency of kidney-yang group, yin-deficiency of liver and kidney group, yang-deficiency of spleen and kidney group and blood stasis due to stagnancy of qi group. There was no significant difference in age distribution, menopausal time and body mass index (BMI, P > 0.05). The bone mineral density (BMD) of lumbar vertebrae from the 2nd to 4th was measured with dual energy X-ray absorptiometry; the estrogen (E2), osteoprotegerin, and receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B ligand levels were detected with ELISA method; the variable inherent relationship of osteoprotegerin, receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B ligand, BMD and E2 in different syndrome types were analyzed with analysis of variance. Results: The contents of osteoprotegerin and receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B ligand in the blood stasis due to stagnancy of qi group were higher than the deficiency of kidney-yang group, yang-deficiency of spleen and kidney group and yin-deficiency of liver and kidney group, and the differences were all significant [(504.71±353.14), (332.03± 323.88), (448.22±304.16), (418.78±382.66) ng/L; (1.348.22±489.87), (1245.62±510.53), (1 258.31±575.71), (1 233.14± 594.32) ng/L, F=1 196.31, 287.54; 137.42, 216.56; 280.39, 342.72; P < 0.01]; the serum levels of E2 and BMD were lower than the deficiency of kidney-yang group with significant differences [(0.48±0.04), (0.68±0.08) g/cm2-; (18.77±9.03), (22.14±8.16) ng/L, F=9.39, 17.93, P < 0.05]. The serum levels of E2 in the yang-deficiency of spleen and kidney group and yin-deficiency of liver and kidney group were lower than the deficiency of kidney-yang group significantly [(19.44± 8.67), (19.41±8.72), (22.14±8.16) ng/L, F=11.23, 11.25, P < 0.05], but there were no significant differences in the comparison of the levels of osteoprotegerin, receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B ligand and BMD (P > 0.05). Conclusion: An imbalance on the system of osteoprotegerin/receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B ligand/receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B has a close relationship with postmenopausal osteoporosis; The change from deficiency of kidney-yang to blood stasis due to stagnancy of qi in the syndrome type may be a critical component in the pathological succession of postmenopausal osteoporosis. The levels of osteoprotegerin and receptor activator of nuclear factor-kappa B ligand may be regarded as the objective examinational indexes to distinct the blood stasis due to stagnancy of qi types with the rest syndrome types.
